[36m(main_task pid=3614448)[0m 📝 Question: What did romans use to clean their teeth?
[36m(main_task pid=3614448)[0m 🤔 Ambiguous: False
[36m(main_task pid=3614448)[0m 🤖 Response: Dental hygiene is the care and improvement of the oral health of the teeth, the dentition, and the surrounding soft tissues. Cleaning one's teeth was a common practice in the modern world and had been for many centuries, with practices going back as far as the Indus Valley Civilization. The teeth were cleaned by rubbing them with the chew-stick (Aglaëa) and bitumen sawdust, and with clumps of wood ash, ashes, salt and the ashes of burnt cattle bone. Soap has been in use since the time of Charlemagne, who commanded the "consuetudinis lapidum et poma beate Marie" to be continued, meaning that powdered wood ash and tallow was to be used in place of the now rare fat from Bees and Lard. Cigars are modern tooth-cleaning methods. In the 1920s or 1930s, after Communion and Bible reading on Sundays, children's hands would be lathered with some bituminous coal before being washed off in the river or at home, where their teeth would be noticeably cleaner. Dr. Everwhite also introduced cotton teeth cleaning balls, and various home treatments.
